           "A naval squadron that rounded Cape Horn, lost most of its men to sickness and still came home with a Spanish treasure galleon.",
           "Anson's squadron rounded Cape Horn in 1741 into months of storms. By the time the survivors limped to the Juan Fernandez Islands in June, the crews of the three ships still afloat had shrunk from 961 men to 335. Gums swelled over teeth, old wounds reopened, men died in their hammocks. Anson pressed on anyway, crossed the Pacific in the *Centurion* and on 20 June 1743 captured the Manila galleon *Nuestra Senora de Covadonga*, carrying 1,313,843 pieces of eight. Britain cheered the silver. A Scottish naval surgeon named James Lind read the death toll instead.",
           "The most famous scurvy disaster in British history, and the book that made Lind start his trial.",
